cument that we follow as part of the  true regulation which is the Animal  Welfare Act and so with that said  surgery and post surgical care  less than a page four paragraphs of  seven paragraphs total giving you your  guidance for working on surgery which I  would hopefully would all agree is a  pretty serious all component and could  have impacts on animal welfare  twenty-five years later we've gone from  less than a page to nine pages and it's  been expanded greatly it talks about the  training it talks about the specifics of  how to perform operating technique how  the functions of the team work it's much  more comprehensive than it was the  training so 25 years ago it was for all  intents and purposes apprentice style  training you with minimal to no skills  were hired into a university or a  company or what have you and someone who  had more experience than you showed you  how to do things your co-workers were  your primary source of any information  and this led to if you will a telephone  tag style of information and as anyone  who's played that party game knows what  you hear five people into that game is  not what was originally set and that  happened a lot and there was also a  piece of this where you were being  trained by someone who hopefully  remember as much as possible about the  person who was being trained who had  trained them who had trained them and  oftentimes you could go back to someone  who was basically inventing it on their  own another thing when it started and  this was both good and bad a vast  majority of the people who perform in  these techniques on a day to day  situation were either veterinary  technicians or people with high school  graduates oh sorry with high school  diplomas  most of the time if you had a more  advanced degree you were overseeing the  studies or you were designing the  studies but you weren't working on the  studies maybe you did that in school as  part of your degree but you got out of  that when you started and so that meant  that the scientific judgment on a study  tended to be in that scientists hands  who was overseeing the project the study  director if you will um conferences  there weren't many that focused on  actual technical skills again they might  spend 30 seconds talking about we did  these technical things but then we went  on to what everyone was at the  conference for which was here's the data  here's the graphs again this is a long  time ago so these were 35 millimeter  slides in a carousel and really at the  time a lass was the group that put this  together and that's changed that's  changed a lot um not so much in the  training it's still very heavily a  hands-on apprentice style of training  but now the average person who comes  into the field has a bachelor's often in  biology or chemistry or lab animal  medicine um but there are a lot of  outside training organizations um not  the least of which is the one hosting  this very seminar and that's important  because if you think about it  25 years ago if you wanted to see a  presentation your company was going to  pay one two three thousand dollars and  take you out of work for one two three  four five days and send you to it now  there are webinars there are events like  this there are online videos there's so  much more information out there oh there  are textbooks that we simply didn't have  back in the day um there are textbooks  on surgery on anesthesia on technical  skills um back in the day we had Pollak  mills in Wayne sports surgery and  technique in the  rat and that's it now  we have online forms you can go and  interact with thousands or tens of  thousands of people in lab animal  community and ask them questions and  many of them will help you this is  amazing so one of the things that that  has led to is there's much more of a  sense of if you will professionalism  there are industry-wide standards we  have metrics and we believe in metrics  the conference's started to focus more  on this technical refinement um again  even things like PubMed knew twenty-five  years ago I spent a lot of time in  University Libraries chasing down  physical papers and then taking the  plastic credit card that I had from that  library going to the photocopier  photocopying whatever articles now if I  need an article I don't want to PubMed I  can go into other search engines and I  can just pull it and read it I used to  spend as so many of us did you could  spend an entire afternoon in a library  if you get three or four papers now I  can pull up almost every paper it can be  in a foreign language and I can  translate it that's huge it gives us so  much more information than we ever had  so what about technology well let's just  talk about a mention um they were just  getting into the field and they were  enormously expensive and rare and  usually they were in human hospitals  because you did not have them in a lab  animal situation so the number of times  that we would do MRI on a rabbit for  instance where at one o'clock in the  morning because that was the only time  it wasn't in use we would take it to the  University Hospital and get our scans  done and then disinfect the entire area  so that patients could come in at 6  o'clock that same morn um because it was  rare and because it was expensive we  didn't use it very  um for drug delivery we had infusion  technologies and those were often human  equipment human catheters um the  technology was honestly pretty crude  wonderful if you like rebuilding things  on the fly in an emergency but not very  good for out-of-the-box  um even or caging it was unwieldy it  wasn't designed for our type of use it  lacked ergonomics they were wonderful  fortunately they did a wonderful job  protecting our lab animals they often  didn't do such a great job protecting us  working with them but let's take imaging  for example um now it's expensive but  it's mature ok because most of our lab  animal species are small you know you're  putting it into a six or seven pound  rabbit where you're putting it into a  250 gram rat the equipment that we had  25 years ago was designed at the  smallest for pediatric patients so a 40  50 pound human child  now there's equipment that's actually  designed for the size and weight ranges  that we work in and it's accepted by the  scientific community you have to do a  lot of if you will selling back in the  day to bring these through now it's  accepted and it's understood and there's  information and every time you do a  study with them you're not a pioneer um  and they're new high resolution  ultrasounds their new MRI technologies  on so on and I'll set a stage a little  bit for a brief video but this video is  just to give you an idea  this isn't 25 years ago this is 15 years  ago and they were some of the first  ultrasound scans that I did where things  really started to come together where  when you can image a the fetal heart of  a rat in utero or you can find  complications and genetic abnormalities  in larger species that we just never  were able to do before I really think  that this imaging really came into its  own in the early 2000s  you  all right and again infusion for drug  delivery and our skill to do it has  kicked in all that up in the upper right  hand corner is a mouse femoral artery so  the black suture that you see up at the  top is a little bit smaller than the  diameter of human hair so we've learned  a lot along the way and again our  practices and her methodology are now  standardized and there are people who  make much better specialized equipment  you know the access technologies and the  SI eye and the instep and so on they've  brought real refinement to the  technologies that we have even caging  there's great of choice the ergonomics  are better for us as well as the lab  animals on enrichment is better so that  we can improve the quality of the  animals experience and now they're  really designed for us to work with them  and they're not just simply housing and  then other thing we understand and more  importantly it is generally accepted so  many of these basic concepts now and  that's a huge improvement so we  understand things like the  appropriateness of true asepsis and  analgesia not just for the animals  welfare although frankly I think that  may be the most important but also  because we don't want all of the  cascading effects on our study data we  understand that animals dard pain and  distress so much better there's so much  more study so now we can pick up on  subtle signs where we just didn't have  that information before and these have  been in partnership with human pediatric  nurses and the Nick Hughes and what have  you who also have patients who can't  speak and articulate and we've learned  from them and in some cases they've  learned from us and those technical  skills as I said at the very beginning  have becomes  standardized across the field we  understand things like fair housing for  primates and better enrichment and the  effects of ambient noise and how to  provide a better environment around our  lab animals the things that have changed  so I'm being a little facetious here but  ergonomics and safety um in 94 we didn't  think about you know yes we wore basic  PPE um we tried not to get injured um  but we didn't do a great job on and in  real terms that's why you don't see a  lot of people from when I started still  doing hands-on lab animal because our  economics were never a focus and we  injured ourselves wrists shoulders backs  eyes  necks we put ourselves in awkward  positions and it didn't do us a whole  heck of a lot of good so now in part  because as our level of work has  increased the cost of training someone  to be able to do it has gone up so you  really want to protect them a lot more  staff retention has become more valuable  you know when I started and I was  working with non-human primates in the  middle picture we were wearing those and  gloves and we were working with  conscious primates and if you were to  win if you will the reverse lottery  where the primate that [ __ ] you  happened to have herpes B you could die  you could be on a cycle of air or some  other on a suppressant for the rest of  your life you know we thought it was  amazing when we started getting Kevlar  sleeves over in the left um now you  wouldn't dream of working with them like  that and not just the safety of the  people working with them but the safety  of the primates it's better we have  better materials we know  handle them so that they're better  protected while we're working that has  been a tremendous change 11 I kind of  like this particular meme if you will so  a lot has gotten better but we have  become I think more hyper focused and  our studies are more streamlined and  that can be all to the good  but as our studies have become more  expensive and complicated we've reduced  the number of animals on study the  studies themselves are harder to conduct  so we're less prone and less willing to  run them the skill level is higher and  the actual skill that we need to have  high quality data is even higher than  that and there's an attractiveness of  expensive technology but that adds to  the cost which again goes on to reduce  if you will the instances of doing these  and also we focus because there's so  many more publications we tend to accept  the publications on critically and just  follow them so I'll leave you with just  a few things to think about that I think  have lost their emphasis over the last  quarter century and they deserve a  comeback and one is gather as much data  as you can something as simple as when  your animal is humanely euthanized at  the end of the study look at everything  if you don't look you're not going to  see things and I can tell you as so many  of my colleagues can as well that after  1 to 3 decades of this you get surprised  you get surprised a lot that surprise is  information that you would not have had  otherwise but if you're not looking if  you were trying to reduce the footprint  of your studies then you're losing data  and it's a terrible thing to find out  about that missing piece of data when  for instance you were  hound fails in clinical trials because  you would have seen it but you weren't  looking um don't discount human  interaction that the internet is  wonderful again I remember when I got on  the internet at work for the very first  time and trust me there was far less on  it back then but don't focus on looking  at things electronically talk to people  the wonderful thing about technology now  is we can use that technology to then  reach out to the people who generated it  you can email the authors of papers you  can call them many of them will talk to  you  who doesn't like talking about their  work and what they do and then you can  get the real information but it has  become so easy to take a three sentence  reply on a listserv or read seventeen  papers talk to the people who did the  work and learn what they know and be  often surprised by how much wasn't in  the information that you had and then  another piece that I would like to point  out I've been training surgeons for  about twenty years now and I will tell  you that some of the best surgeons I  have ever trained some of the best lab  animal technicians I have ever trained  at high school diplomas came from other  careers but they were smart and they  were capable and the amazing thing as  I've told so many of my direct reports  over the years is we can read the exact  same books that everyone else can you  can educate yourself at work and I would  put out that when you're surrounded by  truly smarter scientists you can learn a  lot from them possibly faster so don't  discount these people don't kind of put  aside these people who have all the  skills and all the drive and all the  determination and all the intelligence  just  because they don't fit a certain  criteria and that I think is maybe where  we have lost the most in this field over  the last 25 years and my hope is that we  can get back to looking at the quality  of the people and bringing those  exceptional people in and with that I'm  going to say thank you again if you have  any questions please feel free to email  them in and I will be more than happy to  answer them but with that thank you so  much
 

